  "completion": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to forecast the probability that by certain deadlines, a solution to at least one of the remaining Millennium Prize Problems (MPPs) will be achieved, with AI contributing a new mathematical argument. The MPPs in question are Birch, Hodge, Navier-Stokes, P vs NP, Riemann, and Yang-Mills. The forecast is from 2025, so the first deadline is the end of 2026, then 2030, 2035, 2040, and 2050.\n\nFirst, I should recall what each of these problems is and their prior progress. For example, P vs NP is a major question in computer science, with no proof yet, but many people have worked on it. The Riemann Hypothesis has partial progress but hasn't been solved. Navier-Stokes equations are about fluid dynamics, and while there's some work, existence and smoothness are still open. Hodge and Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer are in algebraic geometry and number theory, respectively. Yang-Mills is about quantum physics.\n\nEach requires significant insight. Solving them could take decades. Now, the key part is the AI contribution. Even if someone claims a solution, the solution must have a novel argument or proof step contributed by AI. Substantial collaboration between humans and AI might count, like if the AI proposes a conjecture or suggests a new approach that the human then develops. But partial progress isn't enough; it has to be a complete solution.\n\nNow, considering the timeline. By 2026? Probably very low. These are long-standing problems. But AI in 2025 is already advanced, and with more time, it might lead to some breakthroughs. Then, 2030 is 5 years away. Maybe there's a 10% chance? 2035 is another 5 years, perhaps 20%? 2040 maybe 30%, and 2050 could be higher, like 40% or maybe 50%?\n\nBut wait, the probabilities need to be non-decreasing. So each later date should have a higher or equal probability. Also, the AI's role needs to be identifiable. If a mathematician writes down a proof that they got a hint from an AI, but the AI's contribution is just one step, perhaps that counts. But how realistic is it for AI to contribute something substantial enough? Well, AI has made some progress in mathematics, like in proving some theorems or guiding research, but major open problems?\n\nFor example, the Riemann Hypothesis: AI maybe could find patterns in zeta zeros or suggest a new approach. But it's risky. If we think it's possible for AI to contribute meaningfully, then each year raises the chance. But the MPPs are very hard. Maybe the probabilities start low and rise slowly. Let's see, for 2026, 0.02 chance? 2030: 0.05, 2035: 0.1, 2040: 0.2, 2050: 0.35. That seems gradually increasing. But maybe even lower. For example, maybe 0.01, 0.02, 0.05, 0.1, 0.2. Maybe. But considering AI's capabilities improving rapidly, perhaps 2040 is 0.2, 2050 is 0.5. But not sure. Also, I need to make sure the probabilities are non-decreasing. Each next year's prob is higher than the previous.\n\nBut I have to remember these are very hard problems. To solve one might take until 2030 is low. By 2050, maybe 30% chance? So maybe:\n\n2026: 0.01\n\n2030: 0.05\n\n2035: 0.15\n\n2040: 0.25\n\n2050: 0.40\n\nThat's non-decreasing. Alternatively, maybe even lower. But the event requires AI's contribution. So if AI becomes better in the future, perhaps by 2050 there's a better chance. However, even a small chance each year adds up. For instance, if each year there's a 1% chance of a solution with AI, then over 25 years (2026 to 2050) it's cumulative, but since we need all those years to add up, perhaps the probabilities would rise more. But maybe the chance per year is lower.\n\nGiven the need to be non-decreasing, I think a reasonable possibility is that the probabilities go from low to higher but maybe not too high. For each deadline, maybe:\n\n2026: 0.01\n\n2030: 0.02\n\n2035: 0.05\n\n2040: 0.1\n\n2050: 0.2\n\nThat seems plausible. But some might argue that the probabilities should rise faster as AI becomes more capable. However, the problems are so hard, even with AI, maybe they take a while. Also, the AI's contribution is not just being available, but that it actually provides a novel proof step. It may or may not, but depending on the problem.\n\nAlternatively, if I think that the median forecast is maybe a 30% chance by 2040 and 50% by 2050, but that might overestimate. Given the considered uncertainty, maybe the probabilities are low for each date but gradually increase. The JSON must have exactly the keys 2026 up to 2050.\n\nSo my final probabilities would be something like:\n\n{\n  \"2026\": 0.01,\n  \"2030\": 0.02,\n  \"2035\": 0.05,\n  \"2040\": 0.1,\n  \"2050\": 0.2\n}\n</think>\n\n{\n  \"2026\": 0.01,\n  \"2030\": 0.02,\n  \"2035\": 0.05,\n  \"2040\": 0.1,\n  \"2050\": 0.2\n}",
